goals.metric:
RELIABILITY — the suite produces a verdict on every run: skips/no-result from 357 of 769 (Q2 2026) down to <[5]%, with “automated” = runs green ([3]-stable-pass);

GROWTH — ESCALATING run-rate, not flat: +[380] (Q3, committed) → +[550] (Q4) → +[600]/quarter (2027), because the one-time costs are already paid; 12-month bar = 100% of the AUTOMATABLE base done (sized by the October regression plan), expected ~[70–75]% of all TCs ≈ [2,900] of the FROZEN Jul-2026 base (3,693 — frozen on purpose: the team also creates ~800 new TCs/quarter, a moving denominator would hide progress); 

QUALITY SIGNAL — bugs found BY the automation suite (label QA-CRM_Automation): ≥[10]/quarter (Q2 actual: 6). Every release/deploy followed by an automated run with a published Allure pass/fail within [1] business day; zero releases shipped without the automated check.

Two layers: 
SINGLE-USER TIMINGS — already exist (Allure per-build timing comparison, proven in CRM-11415): put thresholds on them, extend to the paths users complain about (dashboards, reports, list views), watch them every release;
LOAD & SCALE — still missing: perf env with production-like data volumes (the 2025 blocker), concurrency on top-[10] critical flows. Success = monthly perf report with trend vs baseline AND perf regressions found by QA BEFORE users report them — CRM-11415 is the documented counter-example to beat (users reported the slowness; QA verified the fix afterwards).
